  "headline": "Does Drinking Five or More Cups of Coffee Daily Significantly Reduce the Risk of Liver Cancer and Cirrhosis?",
  "dek": "New research suggests that coffee consumption may offer significant protection against liver diseases, including cancer and cirrhosis.",
  "body_markdown": "**Coffee Consumption Linked to Lower Liver Disease Risks**

A growing body of evidence indicates that drinking five or more cups of coffee daily could significantly reduce the risk of developing liver cancer by up to 47%. This finding comes from a large-scale study analyzing data on nearly 355,000 adults over a period of about 13 years. The results showed that individuals who consumed this amount of coffee had a notably lower incidence of liver cancer compared to non-coffee drinkers.

Interestingly, these benefits were not limited to regular coffee alone. Decaffeinated coffee was found to provide nearly the same level of protection for the liver as caffeinated varieties. Researchers suggest that compounds other than caffeine—such as antioxidants and anti-inflammatory agents—are likely responsible for these health benefits.

**Liver Health Benefits Across Coffee Types**

Studies have also shown that both regular and decaf coffee consumers experienced improved liver function markers and reduced inflammation levels in their livers. MRI scans and blood tests revealed less fat accumulation, scarring, and overall damage in individuals who regularly drank coffee. These findings were consistent across multiple studies conducted by institutions such as Cedars-Sinai Medical Center and UK Biobank.

Moreover, research has found that drinking one to two cups of coffee daily can lower the risk of cirrhosis by 20%, while consuming five or more cups per day may reduce this risk even further—by up to 32%. This protective effect was observed regardless of whether the coffee contained caffeine or not, reinforcing the idea that other components within coffee contribute significantly to liver health.

**Coffee’s Broader Health Impacts**

Beyond its impact on liver disease, coffee consumption is also associated with a reduced risk of chronic diseases in general. Research published earlier this year linked regular coffee intake to increased longevity and better overall health outcomes. These findings highlight the potential role of coffee as part of a healthy lifestyle, offering benefits beyond just liver protection.

In conclusion, while more research is needed to fully understand all mechanisms behind these effects, current evidence strongly supports the notion that drinking five or more cups of coffee daily may offer substantial protective benefits for the liver, including reducing the risks of cancer and cirrhosis.",
